 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Composition via Convolution (Power Form)](1591-composition-via-convolution-power-form.md)
-Evaluates the composition of a trivariate polynomial field with a linear ray parameterization. It solves the problem of efficient numerical evaluation in direct volume ray casting by using convolution operators to reduce computation and register usage compared to naive substitution. Unlike [Feed-Backward Reconstruction](2433-feed-backward-reconstruction.md), which focuses on reconstruction filters, this method specifically optimizes the algebraic evaluation of the polynomial composition itself. Choose this when the volume data is represented as trivariate polynomials and high-order continuity is required.
+Evaluates the composition of a trivariate polynomial field with a linear ray parameterization. It solves the problem of efficient numerical evaluation in direct volume ray casting by using convolution operators to reduce computation and register usage compared to naive substitution [G]. Unlike [Feed-Backward Reconstruction](2433-feed-backward-reconstruction.md), which focuses on reconstruction filters, this method specifically optimizes the algebraic evaluation of the polynomial composition itself. Choose this when the volume data is represented as trivariate polynomials and high-order continuity is required [G].
 
 ### [Feed-Backward Reconstruction](2433-feed-backward-reconstruction.md)
-A spatial-domain convolution method that computes each output sample by gathering weighted contributions from neighboring input samples. It solves the problem of resampling discrete volume data onto a finer or different grid by centering the reconstruction kernel at the output location and summing the input values weighted by the kernel function. It is distinguished from its sibling [Composition via Convolution (Power Form)](1591-composition-via-convolution-power-form.md) by its explicit spatial gathering loop structure, which accesses each output sample once and each input sample multiple times, contrasting with feed-forward approaches that distribute input energy to multiple outputs. Choose this for standard resampling of discrete voxel grids.
